{
  "instrument": {
    "state": "Act on sequential daily scanning, metadata-review and rights-check counts, together with the limited sample of folder histories. The supplied facts establish backlogs, variable folder size and completeness, and differing volunteer schedules; they do not establish misdated or missing entries. Treat uncertain dates, omitted reporting days and duplicate entries as hypotheses to check. Identify any independently dated records and overlapping histories available as references. Assume shared work events may leave recognizable patterns in related records, while allowing metadata and rights activity to occur later than scanning.",
    "operation": "Preserve the original records and construct ordered daily sequences using consistent definitions of folders entering, completing and awaiting each stage. Distinguish a recorded zero from an absent report. Select sampled histories across stages, folder sizes and completeness levels where those attributes are available; document gaps in coverage. First compare records describing the same events, such as daily scanning completions and dated scanning entries in folder histories. Shift uncertain sequences by whole reporting days and look for distinctive runs of peaks, dips and pauses that correspond across overlapping records. Check candidate placements against independently supported dates and additional histories, rather than accepting a single matching peak. Where a candidate alignment reveals an apparent missing day, duplicate count or misplaced entry, inspect the associated histories before proposing a correction. Compare different workflow stages only when folder identities or histories establish the relationship; preserve genuine processing delays. If several alignments fit, references conflict or overlap is insufficient, stop assigning exact dates, retain the alternatives and seek specifically dated histories that could distinguish them.",
    "signal": "The proposed procedure would yield useful evidence if one alignment repeatedly matches distinctive multi-day patterns across overlapping records, respects independent date anchors and remains consistent when checked against histories not used to select it. A suspected omission, duplication or date error would gain support if the aligned records expose a specific inconsistency that folder histories also explain. Persistent mismatches, equally plausible shifts or matches requiring unexplained changes in processing delays would instead signal unresolved chronology. These are prospective observations, not findings already established for this project.",
    "inference": "A supported alignment would justify assigning or checking reporting dates for particular recorded events and extending the checked timeline through overlapping records. Verified corrections could then support estimates of backlog age and stage-specific completions, with uncertainty retained for unresolved entries. Use that reconstructed timeline and sampled histories to distinguish waiting from active processing where the evidence permits. For the next two weeks, provisionally assign suitably qualified, available volunteers to the stages whose backlogs constrain folders becoming exhibition-ready, accounting for folder complexity and prerequisites. Track daily arrivals and completions and revisit assignments if the backlog persists or shifts stages. To assess the ten-week promise, combine checked completion rates with remaining work, sampled complexity, anticipated attendance and required rights clearance; explicitly label those inputs as estimates or assumptions. If a plausible range of completion scenarios falls short of 4,000 ready folders, prepare a reduced exhibition scope tied to cleared, completed folders; if scenarios straddle the deadline, retain conditional scope options and gather the histories or staffing information most likely to resolve the decision.",
    "limit": "Volunteer schedules, batching, folder complexity, incomplete documentation and genuine review or rights delays can disrupt shared patterns. Short or repetitive weekly patterns, counting errors, incorrect reference dates and insufficient overlap can create false alignments. Agreement between records copied from one another is not independent corroboration. Do not force stage counts to match, equate an absent report with zero work, or infer processing time from a reporting-date shift. Limited histories may omit difficult folders, and aggregate daily counts may conceal repeat work or incompatible counting units. Chronological agreement supports event placement; it does not by itself establish bottleneck causes, future capacity, metadata accuracy or legal clearance. Where these uncertainties materially change assignments or scope, use bounded scenarios and targeted follow-up checks rather than a single completion forecast."
  }
}
